00:00:11.869 --> 00:00:20.100
COVID-19, a virus orders of magnitude smaller
than a particle of dust, has turned daily

00:00:20.100 --> 00:00:22.860
life on earth into something deeply unfamiliar.

00:00:22.860 --> 00:00:28.610
In the ten or so months since it first emerged,
the virus has settled at a steady clip of

00:00:28.610 --> 00:00:33.670
killing 5,000 people per day, or nearly a
million total, currently.

00:00:33.670 --> 00:00:38.190
It has played havoc with the world economy,
plunging the globe into the worst economic

00:00:38.190 --> 00:00:40.410
crisis since World War Two.

00:00:40.410 --> 00:00:45.850
It has interrupted an era of mounting globalization,
and pushed us into a more insular, nationalistic

00:00:45.850 --> 00:00:46.850
state.

00:00:46.850 --> 00:00:52.769
The effects of this virus are truly all encompassing
and consistently dreadful, but time has bestowed

00:00:52.769 --> 00:00:55.989
upon us a reward for our patience.

00:00:55.989 --> 00:01:01.170
We are potentially nearing the beginning of
the end, meaning we could now be at the end

00:01:01.170 --> 00:01:02.601
of the middle.

00:01:02.601 --> 00:01:06.510
That end of this virus will take far longer
than the beginning—we’re likely far from

00:01:06.510 --> 00:01:12.080
halfway through the pandemic in terms of time—but
the slow decline in dreadfulness is potentially

00:01:12.080 --> 00:01:13.370
on the horizon.

00:01:13.370 --> 00:01:17.580
That’s because a vaccine is coming.

00:01:17.580 --> 00:01:21.740
There was previously a period of doubt on
whether an effective vaccine could be developed,

00:01:21.740 --> 00:01:26.860
it’s never a sure shot, but all indications
currently suggest, given the unprecedented

00:01:26.860 --> 00:01:31.670
extent of scientific effort, that it is indeed
coming.

00:01:31.670 --> 00:01:36.360
The only and paramount question is when.

00:01:36.360 --> 00:01:41.930
Right now, in September, 2020, all attention
is focused on the late-October, early-November

00:01:41.930 --> 00:01:42.930
timeframe.

00:01:42.930 --> 00:01:47.420
That’s the earliest possible moment when
any of the frontrunner vaccines, from major

00:01:47.420 --> 00:01:53.200
manufacturers including AstraZeneca, Pfizer,
and Moderna, could enter regulatory review.

00:01:53.200 --> 00:01:58.170
If all the data confidently point to the vaccine
exhibiting an acceptable level of safety and

00:01:58.170 --> 00:02:02.990
effectiveness, it is possible that one of
these will then be granted emergency use authorization

00:02:02.990 --> 00:02:07.490
by the American FDA, or its equivalent by
other major national regulators.

00:02:07.490 --> 00:02:12.650
It’s worth noting that this is far from
a sure-shot, but experts do say that having

00:02:12.650 --> 00:02:17.320
a vaccine in 2020 is a less-than-long-shot
possibility.

00:02:17.320 --> 00:02:24.230
However, it is almost certain that you will
not be injected by a COVID-19 vaccine in the

00:02:24.230 --> 00:02:26.290
year 2020.

00:02:26.290 --> 00:02:31.920
It is possible, but for the average person,
when you actually get a dose of a COVID vaccine

00:02:31.920 --> 00:02:37.620
has much less to do with when it’s approved,
and more with how fast it is manufactured

00:02:37.620 --> 00:02:38.940
and distributed.

00:02:38.940 --> 00:02:44.260
The full effects of the pandemic—health,
social, and economic—will not end until

00:02:44.260 --> 00:02:47.500
a critical mass of people are vaccinated.

00:02:47.500 --> 00:02:52.100
That means that when we get back to normal
life has much more to do with when the last

00:02:52.100 --> 00:02:55.380
person is vaccinated, rather than the first.

00:02:55.380 --> 00:03:00.819
This is why some have said that the fast distribution
of a vaccine will be an even greater challenge

00:03:00.819 --> 00:03:02.660
than the development itself.

00:03:02.660 --> 00:03:07.800
This process, this challenge of getting the
majority of the world's population vaccinated,

00:03:07.800 --> 00:03:12.840
can quite legitimately be described as the
most daunting and consequential single logistical

00:03:12.840 --> 00:03:14.120
challenge ever.

00:03:14.120 --> 00:03:20.069
The stakes have never been higher, but the
challenge has also never been greater.

00:03:20.069 --> 00:03:22.240
Let's look at why.

00:03:22.240 --> 00:03:24.770
First and foremost, it's a scale problem.

00:03:24.770 --> 00:03:29.470
Shipping and administering one vaccine to
one person is not difficult, but shipping

00:03:29.470 --> 00:03:33.420
and administering five billion vaccines to
five billion people is.

00:03:33.420 --> 00:03:38.180
That’s because there just isn't enough room
in our world's logistics network, as it is

00:03:38.180 --> 00:03:40.160
now, to do that.

00:03:40.160 --> 00:03:44.630
Making it even tougher, there’s more chance
than not, based on the current leading candidates,

00:03:44.630 --> 00:03:49.770
that the first major vaccine to be approved
will require two doses administered 21 or

00:03:49.770 --> 00:03:51.800
28 days apart.

00:03:51.800 --> 00:03:54.370
That doubles the challenge.

00:03:54.370 --> 00:03:58.680
Distributing ten or more billion doses of
a vaccine would be difficult enough in normal

00:03:58.680 --> 00:04:02.110
times, but these are far from normal times.

00:04:02.110 --> 00:04:06.980
With international travel all but nonexistent—currently
sitting at more than 90% down—passenger

00:04:06.980 --> 00:04:10.739
airlines have dramatically cut back their
international route networks.

00:04:10.739 --> 00:04:16.010
Now, this is an issue because traditionally,
some 50% of pharmaceuticals are shipped in

00:04:16.010 --> 00:04:20.739
the belly-hold of passenger aircraft, rather
than on dedicated freighters, as this can

00:04:20.739 --> 00:04:24.290
help get them closer to their final destination
faster.

00:04:24.290 --> 00:04:30.130
With limited passenger service, overall cargo
capacity is down about 30%, while cargo demand

00:04:30.130 --> 00:04:34.910
is only about 15% down, meaning there is already
a supply crunch.

00:04:34.910 --> 00:04:39.610
This has led to existing dedicated freighter
aircraft being used more, additional dedicated

00:04:39.610 --> 00:04:43.840
freighters coming into service, but still,
due to constrained supply, a dramatic increase

00:04:43.840 --> 00:04:45.639
in shipping costs.

00:04:45.639 --> 00:04:50.500
Even already, before the COVID vaccine comes
into the global logistics network, pharmaceutical

00:04:50.500 --> 00:04:54.600
manufacturers have had to come up with new
techniques to ship their goods including,

00:04:54.600 --> 00:04:58.210
for example, flying their product to where
shipping rates are cheaper and trucking them

00:04:58.210 --> 00:05:01.080
to their final destination or charteri ng
private planes.

00:05:01.080 --> 00:05:05.729
Of course, the world already experienced a
massive logistics challenge this year with

00:05:05.729 --> 00:05:09.800
the distribution of personal protective equipment,
and shippers were able to scale up for that

00:05:09.800 --> 00:05:14.420
reasonably successfully, but there’s one
simple reason why it won’t be so easy this

00:05:14.420 --> 00:05:17.910
time around: the cold chain.

00:05:17.910 --> 00:05:20.280
Vaccines are temperature-sensitive.

00:05:20.280 --> 00:05:24.640
Most all of them have to be stored in a highly
precise, climate controlled environment in

00:05:24.640 --> 00:05:27.060
order to stay safe and effective.

00:05:27.060 --> 00:05:31.550
So, the cold chain is the supply chain suited
to shipping goods that need to be kept cold

00:05:31.550 --> 00:05:35.240
or frozen, such as meat, chemicals, and pharmaceuticals.

00:05:35.240 --> 00:05:39.660
Of course, the difficulty with the cold chain
is that everything used has to be specialized—the

00:05:39.660 --> 00:05:45.050
origin warehouse, the truck, the plane, the
en-route warehouse, and the final destination

00:05:45.050 --> 00:05:48.620
each need to have the equipment to keep the
goods at the specified temperature or the

00:05:48.620 --> 00:05:52.460
goods have to be shipped in packaging that
keeps them at the specified temperature.

00:05:52.460 --> 00:05:57.540
Both options are complicated and complication
compounded by other complication is what makes

00:05:57.540 --> 00:06:00.440
a true logistical challenge.

00:06:00.440 --> 00:06:02.640
It doesn’t end there, though.

00:06:02.640 --> 00:06:08.540
One of the leading vaccine candidates, Pfizer’s
BNT162b2, must be stored and transported at

00:06:08.540 --> 00:06:17.050
around -90 °F, or -68 °C. That is far, far
colder than the requirements of most vaccines,

00:06:17.050 --> 00:06:21.770
which can ship either at refrigerated temperatures
or a wider range of freezing temperatures.

00:06:21.770 --> 00:06:26.350
A thermal requirement such as this is a whole
different ball-game, and it means that they'll

00:06:26.350 --> 00:06:31.100
be able to use only a subset of cold chain
infrastructure, which is already a subset

00:06:31.100 --> 00:06:33.610
of the overall logistics infrastructure.

00:06:33.610 --> 00:06:37.830
Beyond the issue of actually getting such
a vaccine to a distribution site, most hospitals

00:06:37.830 --> 00:06:41.900
don't even have facilities to keep a vaccine
at such a temperature, let alone clinics or

00:06:41.900 --> 00:06:47.460
pharmacies, meaning that even finding places
that can receive this vaccine is a challenge.

00:06:47.460 --> 00:06:51.930
Part of the reason why this obstacle has come
up is that traditionally, vaccines are thoroughly

00:06:51.930 --> 00:06:55.930
tested to determine what sort of storage and
shipping conditions they can endure while

00:06:55.930 --> 00:07:00.110
still staying safe and effective, but with
the breakneck pace of development for these

00:07:00.110 --> 00:07:04.800
COVID-19 vaccines, there likely will not have
been enough time to conduct these tests before

00:07:04.800 --> 00:07:08.690
distribution, meaning they'll have to keep
the standards at what's already proven.

00:07:08.690 --> 00:07:13.800
So, to reiterate, already, a likely vaccine
will only be able to be shipped through a

00:07:13.800 --> 00:07:19.810
subset of a subset of a subset of an already
strained logistics network, to a subset of

00:07:19.810 --> 00:07:24.660
sites, and it'll need to happen many billions
of times, but even the solutions to these

00:07:24.660 --> 00:07:27.750
challenges present additional challenges.

00:07:27.750 --> 00:07:32.080
Pfizer, for example, knowing that the unique
distribution requirements of their vaccine

00:07:32.080 --> 00:07:36.050
could limit its commercial success, has developed
a partial solution.

00:07:36.050 --> 00:07:41.331
It’s essentially a highly specialized stand-alone
box that can, with the use of dry ice, keep

00:07:41.331 --> 00:07:44.990
the vaccine at the required temperature for
up to ten days.

00:07:44.990 --> 00:07:49.069
This means that, hypothetically, the vaccine
could be shipped through a more traditional

00:07:49.069 --> 00:07:54.330
logistics network, however, this box has highly
specific requirements.

00:07:54.330 --> 00:07:58.610
For one, upon arrival, it can only be opened
twice per day, not more than one-minute at

00:07:58.610 --> 00:08:02.509
a time, in order to keep the vaccines at the
required temperature.

00:08:02.509 --> 00:08:06.380
That means that a distribution site would
need to accurately predict how many doses

00:08:06.380 --> 00:08:10.960
they would need in a given day before that
day—if they take out too little, they’d

00:08:10.960 --> 00:08:15.009
be slowing the process by not vaccinating
everyone they can, but if they take out too

00:08:15.009 --> 00:08:19.979
much, they’d have potentially life-saving
vaccines go to waste at the end of the day.

00:08:19.979 --> 00:08:24.270
Also, this box can only carry vaccines in
multiples of a thousand, and keep in mind

00:08:24.270 --> 00:08:26.710
that it only keeps cold for ten days.

00:08:26.710 --> 00:08:31.780
So, how, for example, do you go about getting
the vaccine to a clinic that can only vaccinate

00:08:31.780 --> 00:08:35.729
fifty people in a day—like a rural clinic
in a small town.

00:08:35.729 --> 00:08:41.020
Even if shipping was instantaneous, they could
only vaccinate five-hundred people in ten-days,

00:08:41.020 --> 00:08:43.460
meaning half their vaccines would go bad.

00:08:43.460 --> 00:08:47.790
Pfizer is apparently developing a smaller
temperature-controlled box to solve this problem,

00:08:47.790 --> 00:08:52.610
but that means adding a whole other type of
distribution, which increases complexity,

00:08:52.610 --> 00:08:54.850
which slows the process down.

00:08:54.850 --> 00:09:00.150
In its scenario planning, the American CDC
mapped out a distribution process for if both

00:09:00.150 --> 00:09:05.440
Pfizer and Moderna’s vaccines are approved
and, if this happened, the Pfizer vaccine

00:09:05.440 --> 00:09:09.459
would be directed to only large distribution
sites that would either have the facilities

00:09:09.459 --> 00:09:14.100
for ultra-cold storage, or the capacity to
vaccine enough people that the short shelf-life

00:09:14.100 --> 00:09:16.450
of these vaccines wouldn't be an issue.

00:09:16.450 --> 00:09:21.270
Then, the Moderna vaccine, which only requires
traditional freezer temperatures, would be

00:09:21.270 --> 00:09:25.170
directed to smaller sites with less advanced
facilities.

00:09:25.170 --> 00:09:29.510
This would certainly create a scenario in
the US, and likely elsewhere, where the Pfizer

00:09:29.510 --> 00:09:33.550
vaccine would be primarily distributed to
people in cities, while the Moderna vaccine

00:09:33.550 --> 00:09:36.690
would be the vaccine of choice for rural areas.

00:09:36.690 --> 00:09:41.570
Another challenge that needs to be confronted
is the order of operations for distribution.

00:09:41.570 --> 00:09:45.040
The Coronavirus affects different people in
different ways.

00:09:45.040 --> 00:09:49.120
This means that if we want a vaccine to save
the the most number of lives possible, the

00:09:49.120 --> 00:09:53.680
first person in line should be that who is
most susceptible to COVID-19, and the last

00:09:53.680 --> 00:09:56.970
person in line should be that who is least
susceptible.

00:09:56.970 --> 00:10:01.490
One set of research modeled two scenarios—the
first where, of the first 3 billion doses

00:10:01.490 --> 00:10:05.459
available, 50 high-income countries buy up
2 billion of them.

00:10:05.459 --> 00:10:09.600
The second was where the first 3 billion doses
were distributed to countries proportionally

00:10:09.600 --> 00:10:12.680
based on their population, regardless of wealth.

00:10:12.680 --> 00:10:18.390
In this model, twice as many people died overall
with the first scenario—the wealthy countries

00:10:18.390 --> 00:10:22.190
first scenario—than the equitable, proportional
approach.

00:10:22.190 --> 00:10:27.510
However, for vaccination purposes, it is the
least wealthy countries, arguably those that

00:10:27.510 --> 00:10:31.450
need the vaccine most, that are toughest to
distribute to.

00:10:31.450 --> 00:10:36.270
Poor countries tend to have limited cold chain
infrastructure, let alone normal logistics

00:10:36.270 --> 00:10:40.500
infrastructure, and fragmented, poorly-funded
healthcare systems.

00:10:40.500 --> 00:10:44.700
Distributing and administering a complex vaccine
like Pfizer’s will already be tough enough

00:10:44.700 --> 00:10:49.330
in the wealthy countries like the United States,
but just imagine how difficult it will be

00:10:49.330 --> 00:10:53.970
to get a vaccine from distributor to someone’s
arm in rural Democratic Republic of the Congo,

00:10:53.970 --> 00:10:59.399
for example, in ten days, while keeping it
at -90 °F.

00:10:59.399 --> 00:11:04.959
How soon a COVID vaccine is administered will
quite directly correlate to how many lives

00:11:04.959 --> 00:11:06.250
are saved.

00:11:06.250 --> 00:11:11.100
Every day of delay will, with certainty, lead
to more death and despair.

00:11:11.100 --> 00:11:15.470
The manufacturers are working as fast as they
can, they are already producing millions of

00:11:15.470 --> 00:11:20.580
doses of their vaccines before they’re approved
so they can be distributed as fast as possible,

00:11:20.580 --> 00:11:25.220
and regulatory agencies have been preparing
for months for an unprecedentedly fast review

00:11:25.220 --> 00:11:31.130
process, so it is now truly down to every
member of the global logistics network to

00:11:31.130 --> 00:11:33.160
save lives.

00:11:33.160 --> 00:11:38.870
Of course, many of those that will be involved,
especially once we get to wide-scale distribution,

00:11:38.870 --> 00:11:44.779
will be companies, not governments or nonprofits,
and for them, this presents a massive business

00:11:44.779 --> 00:11:48.660
opportunity—in addition to the positive
public relations potential.

00:11:48.660 --> 00:11:53.930
UPS, for example, has built two freezer farms
in Louisville, Kentucky, and Venlo, Netherlands,

00:11:53.930 --> 00:11:58.120
near their hubs, which can each hold 48,000
doses and can go down to the temperatures

00:11:58.120 --> 00:11:59.560
required for the Pfizer vaccine.

00:11:59.560 --> 00:12:04.560
DHL and FedEx too are reportedly massively
upscaling their cold-chain infrastructure

00:12:04.560 --> 00:12:08.390
to be able to handle some of the surge in
demand, while other lesser known logistics

00:12:08.390 --> 00:12:10.149
companies are too.

00:12:10.149 --> 00:12:15.500
Ultimately, though, its tough to find someone
in-the-know who doesn’t admit that shipping

00:12:15.500 --> 00:12:17.800
will be a bottleneck.

00:12:17.800 --> 00:12:22.690
We already know it’s not going to go perfectly,
so right now, it’s about doing as much work

00:12:22.690 --> 00:12:26.880
as possible to make sure the process goes
as well as it can be.

00:12:26.880 --> 00:12:31.150
This will come down to making sure that as
much planning work as possible is done now,

00:12:31.150 --> 00:12:36.490
before the vaccine is approved, so that distribution
can happen as fast as possible when that does

00:12:36.490 --> 00:12:37.490
happen.

00:12:37.490 --> 00:12:42.500
Now is the time to figure out how and where
the vaccine will be distributed.

00:12:42.500 --> 00:12:47.980
If you are a healthy, relatively young person
thinking selfishly, you should care tremendously

00:12:47.980 --> 00:12:53.050
about the distribution process, because you
are at the back of the line.

00:12:53.050 --> 00:12:57.589
When you get your shot, when you get to start
letting your guard down, has everything to

00:12:57.589 --> 00:13:03.000
do with how fast everyone in front of you
gets theirs, and how fast they get theirs

00:13:03.000 --> 00:13:06.390
will also impact how many of them will die.

00:13:06.390 --> 00:13:10.510
We know this process will not be perfect,
and it wouldn't be reasonable to ask for perfect

00:13:10.510 --> 00:13:15.769
given the complexity of the challenge, but
if we truly put every effort into planning

00:13:15.769 --> 00:13:21.800
and preparing for this now, and create a system
that orders people by need, rather than wealth,

00:13:21.800 --> 00:13:27.010
we can at least know, at the end of this,
that no lives could have been saved if only

00:13:27.010 --> 00:13:29.920
the vaccine got to them faster.

00:13:29.920 --> 00:13:34.399
For our collective consciousness, looking
back far in the future, this moment will be

00:13:34.399 --> 00:13:40.459
defined not by when we get to go back to concerts
or parties or the gym, but rather by how many

00:13:40.459 --> 00:13:47.300
lives we saved when confronting what is hopefully
the greatest global challenge of this generation.

